arXiv:2510.23590v1 [cs.LG] 27 Oct 2025</div>
</div><div class="ltx_page_content">
<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line">
<div id="p1" class="ltx_para"><span id="p1.1" class="ltx_ERROR undefined">\workshoptitle</span>
<p id="p1.2" class="ltx_p">Reliable ML from Unreliable Data</p>
</div>
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">Lightweight Robust Direct Preference Optimization</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Cheol Woo Kim
</span><span id="id1" class="ltx_note ltx_note_frontmatter ltx_thanks_contribution ltx_role_thanks"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_type">thanks: </span>Equal Contribution</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Shresth Verma<sup id="id2" class="ltx_sup">*</sup>
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Mauricio Tec<sup id="id3" class="ltx_sup">*</sup>
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Milind Tambe
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>School of Engineering and Applied Sciences, Harvard University, Boston, MA 02134
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span><span id="id4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">{cwkim, sverma, mauriciogtec, tambe}@g.harvard.edu</span>
</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Direct Preference Optimization (DPO) has become a popular method for fine-tuning large language models (LLMs) due to its stability and simplicity. However, it is also known to be sensitive to noise in the data and prone to overfitting. Recent works have proposed using distributionally robust optimization (DRO) to address potential noise and distributional shift in the data. However, these methods often suffer from excessive conservatism and high computational cost. We propose <span id="abstract1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">DPO-PRO</span> (<span id="abstract1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">DPO</span> with <span id="abstract1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">P</span>reference <span id="abstract1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Ro</span>bustness), a robust fine-tuning algorithm based on DPO which accounts for uncertainty in the preference distribution through a lightweight DRO formulation. Unlike prior DRO-based variants, <span id="abstract1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">DPO-PRO</span> focuses solely on uncertainty in preferences, avoiding unnecessary conservatism and incurring negligible computational overhead. We further show that <span id="abstract1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">DPO-PRO</span> is equivalent to a regularized DPO objective that penalizes model overconfidence under weak preference signals. We evaluate <span id="abstract1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">DPO-PRO</span> on standard alignment benchmarks and a real-world public health task. Experimental results show that our method consistently improves robustness to noisy preference signals compared to existing DPO variants.</p>
  
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Reinforcement learning from human feedback (RLHF) enables training complex reinforcement learning (RL) policies without manually specifying reward functions. A common approach is to first learn a reward function from a preference data data using maximum likelihood estimation. This learned reward is then used to define an RL objective, typically augmented with a KL-penalty term, and optimized using algorithms such as Proximal Policy Optimization (PPO) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>]</cite>. RLHF methods have been successful in fine-tuning large language models (LLMs) to better align with human preferences <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>, <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>, <a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">A popular alternative to the standard two-stage pipeline is to directly optimize the policy using offline preference data, an approach known as Direct Preference Optimization (DPO) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>]</cite>. DPO is generally known to be more stable and easier to train than PPO. However, it is also known to be vulnerable to noise in the training data and prone to overfitting <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>, <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>, <a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">41</a>, <a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">37</a>]</cite>. This issue is further compounded by the growing use of LLM-annotated <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">44</a>, <a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>, <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>, <a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">45</a>]</cite> or reward model–generated <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>, <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>, <a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>, <a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite> preference data in recent work, which may deviate from actual human preferences.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">To address the noise and distributional shifts associated with training data, distributionally robust optimization (DRO) has been applied to DPO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">36</a>, <a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">40</a>, <a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>]</cite>. DRO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>]</cite> optimizes the worst-case expected loss over a predefined set of distributions. While this approach can provide robustness, it can by overly conservative and introduces computational difficulties, as the resulting min–max robust objective for DPO is often intractable.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">To tackle these challenges, we introduce <span id="S1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">DPO-PRO</span> (<span id="S1.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">DPO</span> with <span id="S1.p4.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">P</span>reference <span id="S1.p4.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Ro</span>bustness), a lightweight and distributionally robust enhancement of DPO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>]</cite>. <span id="S1.p4.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">DPO-PRO</span> incorporates uncertainty in the preference distribution using an efficient yet effective DRO formulation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>]</cite>, ensuring that the fine-tuned policy remains reliable under imperfect preference annotation. Unlike prior DRO-based DPO methods, our approach focuses solely on robustness to the preference distribution, rather than the full data-generating process. This avoids excessive conservatism and introduces only negligible computational overhead. We further show that the resulting robust loss can be interpreted as a regularized DPO loss that penalizes model overconfidence and weak preference signals.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate our approach on a range of publicly available standard benchmarks, as well as a real-world public health–related task for LLMs. Our experiments show that <span id="S1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">DPO-PRO</span> is more robust to noisy preference supervision than both vanilla DPO and prior DRO-based methods.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Related Works</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A growing body of work has explored robustness in RLHF and DPO, particularly by leveraging DRO. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">36</a>]</cite> introduce a DRO-based DPO formulation using a KL-divergence-based ambiguity set. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">40</a>]</cite> extend this approach to both KL and Wasserstein distances, while <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>]</cite> explore robustness under total variation distance, applying it to both DPO and standard two-stage RLHF.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In contrast to these DRO-based approaches, other works assume explicit corruption models. For example, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite> assume that preference labels are flipped according to a known noise rate, and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite> model preferences using a Bradley–Terry (BT) framework <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite> with added noise on the reward differences.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite> use a DRO framework to develop adaptive loss functions for reward model learning, but focus is not on distributional robustness. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">43</a>]</cite> introduce robustness by constructing confidence sets over learned reward functions, followed by pessimistic policy optimization.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Broadly, existing robustness techniques fall into two categories: (1) methods relying on strong assumptions about noise models, which may be unrealistic in practice, and (2) DRO-based methods that avoid explicit corruption modeling but hedge against worst-case distributions. As discussed in Section <a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 (Distributionally Robust) DPO ‣ 3 Preliminaries ‣ Lightweight Robust Direct Preference Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>, standard DRO approaches tend to be overly conservative and often require solving challenging min–max optimization problems. For tractability, prior methods rely on heuristic approximations <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">36</a>, <a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">40</a>]</cite>, potentially undermining theoretical guarantees or practical robustness. In contrast, <span id="S2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">DPO-PRO</span> is compatible with standard gradient-based optimization without relying on heuristics, making it practically efficient. It also avoids unnecessary conservatism and still provides robustness guarantees to preference signals.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Preliminaries</h2>

<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Data Distribution</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In DPO, each data point is a tuple <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x,y_{1},y_{2},c)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x,y_{1},y_{2},c)</annotation></semantics></math>. A prompt <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math> is drawn from a distribution <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math>. Given <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>, responses <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{1},y_{2}\in\mathcal{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{1},y_{2}\in\mathcal{Y}</annotation></semantics></math> are sampled independently from a policy <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\pi(\cdot|x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi(\cdot|x)</annotation></semantics></math>. A label <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c\in\{1,-1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c\in\{1,-1\}</annotation></semantics></math> is a Bernoulli random variable indicating whether response <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> is preferred over <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, with preference probability given by <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p^{*}(y_{1}\succ y_{2}|x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>p</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>≻</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p^{*}(y_{1}\succ y_{2}|x)</annotation></semantics></math>. The distribution <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>p</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> represents the ground-truth preference distribution that the user intends the language model to align with.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In summary, the data-generating process involves three components: (1) the prompt distribution, (2) the response distribution, and (3) the preference distribution. This yields the following joint distribution:
<math id="S3.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="P(x,y_{1},y_{2},c)\;=\;\mu(x)\,\pi(y_{1}|x)\,\pi(y_{2}|x)\,\bigl[\mathbb{I}_{\{c=1\}}\,p^{*}(y_{1}\succ y_{2}\mid x)\;+\;\mathbb{I}_{\{c=-1\}}\,p^{*}(y_{2}\succ y_{1}\mid x)\bigr]." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mi>μ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>π</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>π</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">[</mo><msub><mi>𝕀</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></msub><msup><mi>p</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>≻</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>x</mi><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.502em">+</mo><msub><mi>𝕀</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>c</mi><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></msub><msup><mi>p</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>≻</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(x,y_{1},y_{2},c)\;=\;\mu(x)\,\pi(y_{1}|x)\,\pi(y_{2}|x)\,\bigl[\mathbb{I}_{\{c=1\}}\,p^{*}(y_{1}\succ y_{2}\mid x)\;+\;\mathbb{I}_{\{c=-1\}}\,p^{*}(y_{2}\succ y_{1}\mid x)\bigr].</annotation></semantics></math>
We often omit the dependence of <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>p</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> (and other preference distributions) on <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x,y_{1},y_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x,y_{1},y_{2})</annotation></semantics></math> and simply write <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>p</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> instead of <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p^{*}(y_{1}\succ y_{2}|x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>p</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>≻</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p^{*}(y_{1}\succ y_{2}|x)</annotation></semantics></math> when the meaning is clear from context.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>(Distributionally Robust) DPO</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In standard RLHF, the first step is to learn a reward function <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{\phi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{\phi}</annotation></semantics></math> from a fixed dataset <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}</annotation></semantics></math>. This is typically done by minimizing the negative log-likelihood:</p>
<table id="S3.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-\mathbb{E}_{(x,y_{1},y_{2},c)\sim\mathcal{D}}\left[\log\sigma\left(c\cdot\left(R_{\phi}(x,y_{1})-R_{\phi}(x,y_{2})\right)\right)\right]," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∼</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-\mathbb{E}_{(x,y_{1},y_{2},c)\sim\mathcal{D}}\left[\log\sigma\left(c\cdot\left(R_{\phi}(x,y_{1})-R_{\phi}(x,y_{2})\right)\right)\right],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> is the sigmoid function.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Once the reward model is trained, a policy <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> is optimized to maximize the expected reward while remaining close to a reference policy <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{ref}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{ref}</annotation></semantics></math>, typically via PPO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>]</cite>:</p>
<table id="S3.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\max_{\pi_{\theta}}\mathbb{E}_{(x,y)\sim\pi_{\theta}}\left[R_{\phi}(x,y)\right]-\beta\,\mathrm{KL}(\pi_{\theta}\,\|\,\pi_{\mathrm{ref}})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder><mi>max</mi><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub></munder><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mrow><mo>[</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>β</mi><mi>KL</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.337em">∥</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>ref</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\max_{\pi_{\theta}}\mathbb{E}_{(x,y)\sim\pi_{\theta}}\left[R_{\phi}(x,y)\right]-\beta\,\mathrm{KL}(\pi_{\theta}\,\|\,\pi_{\mathrm{ref}}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> is a regularization parameter controlling the strength of the KL penalty.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">DPO simplifies this pipeline by combining the two stages into a single objective that directly optimizes the policy from pairwise preference data. The DPO loss <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{DPO}}(\pi_{\theta})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>DPO</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{DPO}}(\pi_{\theta})</annotation></semantics></math> is given by:</p>
<table id="S3.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-\mathbb{E}_{(x,y_{1},y_{2},c)\sim\mathcal{D}}\!\bigl[\log\sigma\!\bigl(c\,\beta\Bigl(\log\tfrac{\pi_{\theta}(y_{1}|x)}{\pi_{\mathrm{ref}}(y_{1}|x)}-\log\tfrac{\pi_{\theta}(y_{2}|x)}{\pi_{\mathrm{ref}}(y_{2}|x)}\Bigr)\bigr)\bigr]." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∼</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">[</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:0.437em;" width="0.437em"><mi>σ</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>ref</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>ref</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-\mathbb{E}_{(x,y_{1},y_{2},c)\sim\mathcal{D}}\!\bigl[\log\sigma\!\bigl(c\,\beta\Bigl(\log\tfrac{\pi_{\theta}(y_{1}|x)}{\pi_{\mathrm{ref}}(y_{1}|x)}-\log\tfrac{\pi_{\theta}(y_{2}|x)}{\pi_{\mathrm{ref}}(y_{2}|x)}\Bigr)\bigr)\bigr].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">For brevity, we define the per-sample loss as:</p>
<table id="S3.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell_{\theta}(x,y_{1},y_{2},c)=-\log\sigma\!\Bigl(c\,\beta\!\Bigl(\log\tfrac{\pi_{\theta}(y_{1}|x)}{\pi_{\mathrm{ref}}(y_{1}|x)}-\log\tfrac{\pi_{\theta}(y_{2}|x)}{\pi_{\mathrm{ref}}(y_{2}|x)}\Bigr)\Bigr)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">−</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:0.437em;" width="0.437em"><mi>σ</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mpadded style="width:0.448em;" width="0.448em"><mi>β</mi></mpadded><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>ref</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>ref</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell_{\theta}(x,y_{1},y_{2},c)=-\log\sigma\!\Bigl(c\,\beta\!\Bigl(\log\tfrac{\pi_{\theta}(y_{1}|x)}{\pi_{\mathrm{ref}}(y_{1}|x)}-\log\tfrac{\pi_{\theta}(y_{2}|x)}{\pi_{\mathrm{ref}}(y_{2}|x)}\Bigr)\Bigr).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p4.2" class="ltx_p">Using this expression, <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{DPO}}(\pi_{\theta})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>DPO</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{DPO}}(\pi_{\theta})</annotation></semantics></math> is also equivalent to:</p>
<table id="S3.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}_{(x,y_{1},y_{2})\sim\mathcal{D}}[p^{*}\ell_{\theta}(x,y_{1},y_{2},1)+(1-p^{*})\ell_{\theta}(x,y_{1},y_{2},-1)]." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∼</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>p</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}_{(x,y_{1},y_{2})\sim\mathcal{D}}[p^{*}\ell_{\theta}(x,y_{1},y_{2},1)+(1-p^{*})\ell_{\theta}(x,y_{1},y_{2},-1)].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p4.3" class="ltx_p">When the context is clear that the triplet <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x,y_{1},y_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x,y_{1},y_{2})</annotation></semantics></math> and the parameter <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math> is fixed, we also use <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell_{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell_{-1}</annotation></semantics></math> to denote <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell_{\theta}(x,y_{1},y_{2},1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell_{\theta}(x,y_{1},y_{2},1)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell_{\theta}(x,y_{1},y_{2},-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell_{\theta}(x,y_{1},y_{2},-1)</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">As discussed earlier, DPO is vulnerable to overfitting and noise in the data. To address this, recent work has proposed distributionally robust objectives of the form <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\max_{P\in\mathcal{Q}(\mathcal{D})}\mathcal{L}_{{P}}(\pi_{\theta})," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>max</mi><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>P</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\max_{P\in\mathcal{Q}(\mathcal{D})}\mathcal{L}_{{P}}(\pi_{\theta}),</annotation></semantics></math>
where <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Q}(\mathcal{D})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Q}(\mathcal{D})</annotation></semantics></math> denotes an ambiguity set centered around the empirical distribution (data) <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}</annotation></semantics></math> and the loss <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{{P}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{{P}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the DPO loss evaluated under the perturbed distribution <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> rather than the original data distribution <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">40</a>, <a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">36</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Conservatism and Computational Costs</h4>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The above DRO formulation hedges against shifts in the entire joint distribution on <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x,y_{1},y_{2},c)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x,y_{1},y_{2},c)</annotation></semantics></math>. This allows the adversary to assign weights to highly unlikely prompts or responses. The outer minimization must then optimize against losses in these practically irrelevant regions, which might make the update overly conservative and slow improvement on the data the model actually sees. From a computational standpoint, solving the resulting min–max problem can be intractable in practice, and existing methods often rely on approximations that deviate from the original DRO formulation.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>DPO with Preference Robustness</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we introduce <span id="S4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">DPO-PRO</span>, a distributionally robust version of DPO that specifically targets distributional shifts or other forms of noise in the preference distribution.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Uncertainties in the Preference Distribution</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In each data point <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x,y_{1},y_{2},c)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x,y_{1},y_{2},c)</annotation></semantics></math>, the triplet <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x,y_{1},y_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x,y_{1},y_{2})</annotation></semantics></math> represents observed content, which typically comes from a well-understood and controllable data collection process. In general, <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> is drawn from a pool of candidate inputs, and the response pair <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(y_{1},y_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(y_{1},y_{2})</annotation></semantics></math> is generated by the reference policy <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{ref}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{ref}</annotation></semantics></math> (usually a supervised fine-tuned model) for every sampled prompt <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>. As more data is collected, the uncertainty in this part of the data is expected to diminish. Noise in the prompts and responses becomes even less of an issue under iterative DPO methods, which are increasingly adopted in practice <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">39</a>, <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>, <a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">38</a>]</cite>, as the prompt and response pairs are continually updated.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In contrast, we argue that the preference distribution <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c\sim p^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>∼</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c\sim p^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> is inherently noisy in practice, and collecting more data is unlikely to resolve the underlying uncertainty in human preference. Noise can arise from multiple sources. For example, errors can be made by LLM judges or reward models when these proxies are used for annotation. Even when humans provide annotations directly, other sources of noise remain, including human subjectivity, irrational or inconsistent behavior, and temporal variability (e.g., the same annotator providing different judgments at different times). This noise is persistent and cannot be easily addressed through better data curation or increased data volume. In other words, the ground-truth preference distribution <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>p</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> is an idealized and inaccessible object in practice. Therefore, we argue that uncertainty in the preference distribution should be the primary motivation for incorporating robustness into DPO.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We assume that for each prompt and response pair <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x,y_{1},y_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x,y_{1},y_{2})</annotation></semantics></math>, we have access to a (potentially noisy) preference distribution <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q(y_{1}\succ y_{2}|x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>≻</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q(y_{1}\succ y_{2}|x)</annotation></semantics></math>. We apply DRO adjustment, assuming worst-case deviation from <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> within a pre-specified chi-squared divergence ball. Formally, we define the DRO loss <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{DRO}}(\pi_{\theta})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>DRO</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{DRO}}(\pi_{\theta})</annotation></semantics></math> as:</p>
<table id="S4.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}_{(x,y_{1},y_{2})\sim\mathcal{D}}\max_{p\in Q(x,y_{1},y_{2},\rho)}\mathbb{E}_{c\sim p}\Bigl[\ell_{\theta}(x,y_{1},y_{2},c)\Bigr]," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∼</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><munder><mi>max</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}_{(x,y_{1},y_{2})\sim\mathcal{D}}\max_{p\in Q(x,y_{1},y_{2},\rho)}\mathbb{E}_{c\sim p}\Bigl[\ell_{\theta}(x,y_{1},y_{2},c)\Bigr],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p3.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="Q(x,y_{1},y_{2}\rho)=\left\{p:\chi^{2}\big(p\,\|\,q(y_{1}\succ y_{2}\mid x)\big)\leq\rho\right\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mi>ρ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><msup><mi>χ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0.337em">∥</mo><mi>q</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>≻</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q(x,y_{1},y_{2}\rho)=\left\{p:\chi^{2}\big(p\,\|\,q(y_{1}\succ y_{2}\mid x)\big)\leq\rho\right\}</annotation></semantics></math>.
This formulation also resembles a zero-sum Stackelberg game, as noted in prior work <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>, <a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite>, where the optimizer (leader) selects <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math>, and an adversary (follower) chooses a worst-case distribution in response. The goal is to find the optimal <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math>, anticipating that the follower will act adversarially to maximize the loss.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Note that our formulation avoids unnecessary robustification over the joint distribution on <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x,y_{1},y_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x,y_{1},y_{2})</annotation></semantics></math>, and assumes that this part of the data is reliable (i.e., the adversary does not perturb this part of the distribution). Furthermore, unlike standard DRO methods that can add significant computational burden <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>]</cite>, our formulation introduces negligible additional cost as shown next.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>Computing Worst-case Distribution</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Consider a single data point <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x,y_{1},y_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x,y_{1},y_{2})</annotation></semantics></math>. We assume access to an estimate of the probability that one response is preferred over the other: <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q(y_{1}\succ y_{2}|x)\in(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>≻</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q(y_{1}\succ y_{2}|x)\in(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math>. Given this probability <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>, we define the following per-sample optimization problem:</p>
<table id="S4.E2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S4.E2X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E2X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\max_{p\in[0,1]}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><munder><mi>max</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></munder><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\max_{p\in[0,1]}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E2X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle p\,\ell_{\theta}(x,y_{1},y_{2},1)+(1-p)\,\ell_{\theta}(x,y_{1},y_{2},-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle p\,\ell_{\theta}(x,y_{1},y_{2},1)+(1-p)\,\ell_{\theta}(x,y_{1},y_{2},-1)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="2" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S4.E2Xa" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E2Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\text{s.t.}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext>s.t.</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\text{s.t.}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E2Xa.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\frac{(p-q)^{2}}{q(1-q)}\;\leq\;\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">≤</mo><mi>ρ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\frac{(p-q)^{2}}{q(1-q)}\;\leq\;\rho</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.3" class="ltx_p">This problem finds the worst-case distribution within the chi-squared ambiguity set centered at <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\left\{p:\chi^{2}\big(p\,\|\,q(y_{1}\succ y_{2}\mid x)\big)\equiv\frac{(p-q)^{2}}{q(1-q)}\leq\rho\right\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>{</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><msup><mi>χ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0.337em">∥</mo><mi>q</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>≻</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo>≡</mo><mfrac><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo>≤</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mo>}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\{p:\chi^{2}\big(p\,\|\,q(y_{1}\succ y_{2}\mid x)\big)\equiv\frac{(p-q)^{2}}{q(1-q)}\leq\rho\right\}</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{p}(y_{1}\succ y_{2}|x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>≻</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{p}(y_{1}\succ y_{2}|x)</annotation></semantics></math> denote the optimal solution to this problem. Using this, the robust loss <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{DRO}}(\pi_{\theta})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>DRO</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{DRO}}(\pi_{\theta})</annotation></semantics></math> can be equivalently expressed as</p>
<table id="S4.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}_{(x,y_{1},y_{2})\sim\mathcal{D}}\Bigl[\hat{p}\,\ell_{\theta}(x,y_{1},y_{2},1)+(1-\hat{p})\,\ell_{\theta}(x,y_{1},y_{2},-1)\Bigr]" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∼</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}_{(x,y_{1},y_{2})\sim\mathcal{D}}\Bigl[\hat{p}\,\ell_{\theta}(x,y_{1},y_{2},1)+(1-\hat{p})\,\ell_{\theta}(x,y_{1},y_{2},-1)\Bigr]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Problem (<a href="#S4.E2" title="In 4.2 Computing Worst-case Distribution ‣ 4 DPO with Preference Robustness ‣ Lightweight Robust Direct Preference Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>) is a one-dimensional optimization problem with a linear objective, which admits a simple closed-form solution:</p>
<table id="S4.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{p}(y_{1}\succ y_{2}|x)=\begin{cases}\min\left\{1,q+\sqrt{\rho\,q(1-q)}\right\},\ \text{if }\ell_{1}\geq\ell_{-1},\\[6.0pt]
\max\left\{0,q-\sqrt{\rho\,q(1-q)}\right\},\ \text{if }\ell_{1}&lt;\ell_{-1}.\end{cases}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>≻</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>+</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{p}(y_{1}\succ y_{2}|x)=\begin{cases}\min\left\{1,q+\sqrt{\rho\,q(1-q)}\right\},\ \text{if }\ell_{1}\geq\ell_{-1},\\[6.0pt]
\max\left\{0,q-\sqrt{\rho\,q(1-q)}\right\},\ \text{if }\ell_{1}&lt;\ell_{-1}.\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">This closed-form solution can be directly substituted into the loss in Eq (<a href="#S4.E3" title="In 4.2 Computing Worst-case Distribution ‣ 4 DPO with Preference Robustness ‣ Lightweight Robust Direct Preference Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>), resulting in a fully closed-form expression for the DRO-adjusted loss.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We now explain the intuition behind the closed-from worst-case distribution <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{p}</annotation></semantics></math>. Consider the case where <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell_{1}\geq\ell_{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell_{1}\geq\ell_{-1}</annotation></semantics></math>, which implies that under the current policy <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math>, the model assigns a higher likelihood to <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> being preferred over <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>. To increase the loss, the adversary seeks to shift the preference probability <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q(y_{1}\succ y_{2}\mid x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>≻</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q(y_{1}\succ y_{2}\mid x)</annotation></semantics></math> in the opposite direction. That is, it tries to increase <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> to emphasize a preference for <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, which contradicts the model’s belief. Consequently, the worst-case distribution becomes <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q+\sqrt{\rho\,q(1-q)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>+</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q+\sqrt{\rho\,q(1-q)}</annotation></semantics></math>. Since probabilities must remain within the unit interval, we clip the value at 1, yielding the final expression <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\min\left\{1,\;q+\sqrt{\rho\,q(1-q)}\right\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>+</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\min\left\{1,\;q+\sqrt{\rho\,q(1-q)}\right\}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.3 </span>Efficient Optimization of the Loss</h3>

<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The worst-case preference probability <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{p}</annotation></semantics></math>, obtained in closed-form above, can be directly substituted into the per-sample DPO gradient to compute the gradient for <span id="S4.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">DPO-PRO</span>:</p>
<table id="S4.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{p}\,\nabla_{\theta}\ell_{\theta}(x,y_{1},y_{2},1)\;+\;(1-\hat{p})\,\nabla_{\theta}\ell_{\theta}(x,y_{1},y_{2},-1)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.337em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>θ</mi></msub><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.502em">+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.337em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>θ</mi></msub><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{p}\,\nabla_{\theta}\ell_{\theta}(x,y_{1},y_{2},1)\;+\;(1-\hat{p})\,\nabla_{\theta}\ell_{\theta}(x,y_{1},y_{2},-1).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 4.1</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Eq (<a href="#S4.E4" title="In 4.3 Efficient Optimization of the Loss ‣ 4 DPO with Preference Robustness ‣ Lightweight Robust Direct Preference Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>) provides an unbiased gradient estimate of the <span id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">DPO-PRO</span> loss in Eq (<a href="#S4.E1" title="In 4.1 Uncertainties in the Preference Distribution ‣ 4 DPO with Preference Robustness ‣ Lightweight Robust Direct Preference Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>).</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Note that we do not formally differentiate through the inner maximization in Eq (<a href="#S4.E1" title="In 4.1 Uncertainties in the Preference Distribution ‣ 4 DPO with Preference Robustness ‣ Lightweight Robust Direct Preference Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>) with respect to <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math>. However, Danskin’s theorem <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite> justifies our approach. For a fixed <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math>, we may solve the inner maximization and directly substitute the resulting worst-case <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{p}</annotation></semantics></math> into the gradient expression.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We emphasize the resulting DRO gradient is both exact and computationally efficient. Unlike prior work, our approach does not introduce any approximation or heuristic in minimizing the DRO objective. Moreover, our distributional robustness is applied only to the preference distribution <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>, rather than the full data-generating distribution on <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x,y_{1},y_{2},c)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x,y_{1},y_{2},c)</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence, the resulting method is significantly less conservative than earlier DRO-based DPO approaches.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.4 </span>Analysis of the Loss Function</h3>

<div id="S4.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we analyze the robust loss <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{DRO}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>DRO</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{DRO}}</annotation></semantics></math> and show that it is equivalent to the original DPO loss augmented with a regularization term. This interpretation provides insight into how the DRO formulation affects model learning.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 4.2</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The DRO loss <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{DRO}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">DRO</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{DRO}}</annotation></semantics></math> is equivalent to regularizing the original DPO loss <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{DPO}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">DPO</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{DPO}}</annotation></semantics></math> as:</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{DRO}}=\begin{cases}\mathcal{L}_{\text{DPO}}+\min\left\{1-q,\;\sqrt{\rho\,q(1-q)}\right\}(\ell_{1}-\ell_{-1}),&amp;\text{if }\ell_{1}\geq\ell_{-1},\\
\mathcal{L}_{\text{DPO}}+\min\left\{q,\;\sqrt{\rho\,q(1-q)}\right\}(\ell_{-1}-\ell_{1}),&amp;\text{if }\ell_{1}&lt;\ell_{-1}.\end{cases}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">DRO</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">DPO</mtext></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">DPO</mtext></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mi>q</mi><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msqrt><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{DRO}}=\begin{cases}\mathcal{L}_{\text{DPO}}+\min\left\{1-q,\;\sqrt{\rho\,q(1-q)}\right\}(\ell_{1}-\ell_{-1}),&amp;\text{if }\ell_{1}\geq\ell_{-1},\\
\mathcal{L}_{\text{DPO}}+\min\left\{q,\;\sqrt{\rho\,q(1-q)}\right\}(\ell_{-1}-\ell_{1}),&amp;\text{if }\ell_{1}&lt;\ell_{-1}.\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We now analyze the behavior of the regularization term, assuming that <math id="S4.SS4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell_{1}\geq\ell_{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell_{1}\geq\ell_{-1}</annotation></semantics></math>. The opposite case follows symmetric logic.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">(1) Uncertainty-Weighted Coefficient.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The first factor <math id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\min\left\{1-q,\;\sqrt{\rho\,q(1-q)}\right\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\min\left\{1-q,\;\sqrt{\rho\,q(1-q)}\right\}</annotation></semantics></math> penalizes cases where the preference signal is uncertain (i.e., when <math id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> is close to 0.5). This term is largest when <math id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> is near 0.5 and diminishes as <math id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q\to 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q\to 0</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q\to 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q\to 1</annotation></semantics></math>. Its magnitude is modulated by the robustness radius <math id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math>, with larger <math id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math> leading to a stronger penalty. We visualize this uncertainty-weighted coefficient for various <math id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math> values in Figure <a href="#S4.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ (1) Uncertainty-Weighted Coefficient. ‣ 4.4 Analysis of the Loss Function ‣ 4 DPO with Preference Robustness ‣ Lightweight Robust Direct Preference Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2510.23590v1/figures/penalty.png" id="S4.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:238/153;" width="238" height="153" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 1</span>: </span><span id="S4.F1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Visualization of the uncertainty-weighted coefficient for various values of <math id="S4.F1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math>. This coefficient attains its maximum near <math id="S4.F1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q=0.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q=0.5</annotation></semantics></math> and decreases as <math id="S4.F1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q\to 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q\to 0</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S4.F1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q\to 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q\to 1</annotation></semantics></math>. For small values of <math id="S4.F1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math>, the maximum is achieved exactly at <math id="S4.F1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q=0.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q=0.5</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S4.F1.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sqrt{\rho\,q(1-q)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msqrt><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msqrt><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sqrt{\rho\,q(1-q)}</annotation></semantics></math> peaks. However, for larger <math id="S4.F1.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math>, the maximum occurs at the intersection point between the curves <math id="S4.F1.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1-q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1-q</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.F1.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sqrt{\rho\,q(1-q)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msqrt><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msqrt><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sqrt{\rho\,q(1-q)}</annotation></semantics></math>. This is because <math id="S4.F1.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q+\sqrt{\rho\,q(1-q)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>+</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q+\sqrt{\rho\,q(1-q)}</annotation></semantics></math> (the worst-case distribution under the chi-squared divergence constraint) may exceed 1 when <math id="S4.F1.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math> is large, causing the worst-case distribution <math id="S4.F1.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo mathsize="1.110em">^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{p}</annotation></semantics></math> to be clipped at 1. In such cases, the adversary’s perturbation <math id="S4.F1.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{p}-q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo mathsize="1.110em">^</mo></mover><mo>−</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{p}-q</annotation></semantics></math> can be greater when <math id="S4.F1.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q&lt;0.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q&lt;0.5</annotation></semantics></math>, leading the penalty term to peak at a value of <math id="S4.F1.m32" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> smaller than 0.5.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">(2) Model Confidence.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The second factor <math id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\ell_{1}-\ell_{-1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\ell_{1}-\ell_{-1})</annotation></semantics></math> reflects the log-odds of the current model’s preference for <math id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> over <math id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>. A large value indicates strong model confidence. This term increases when the model becomes more certain about the preference, regardless of the ground-truth label.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">(3) Combined Effect.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The regularization term penalizes the model for being overly confident in its preferences when the preference signal <math id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> is ambiguous. In contrast, when the signal is strong (i.e., <math id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> is close to 0 or 1), the penalty diminishes, allowing the model to express stronger preferences. In effect, the DRO loss encourages calibrated learning: the model is allowed to be confident only when the preference signal is also confident.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.5 </span>Obtaining the Soft Score <math id="S4.SS5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> in Practice</h3>

<div id="S4.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The DRO adjustment described above requires a soft score <math id="S4.SS5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q(y_{1}\succ y_{2}\mid x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>≻</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q(y_{1}\succ y_{2}\mid x)</annotation></semantics></math>, rather than a binary annotation for each prompt and response pair <math id="S4.SS5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x,y_{1},y_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x,y_{1},y_{2})</annotation></semantics></math>, which is the typical format in preference learning. Fortunately, such soft scores are increasingly available in modern DPO pipelines.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">If the dataset contains multiple binary annotations for the same pair (e.g., from different annotators), <math id="S4.SS5.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q(y_{1}\succ y_{2}\mid x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>≻</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q(y_{1}\succ y_{2}\mid x)</annotation></semantics></math> can be estimated by averaging the votes. When LLM judges are used, as in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">44</a>, <a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>, <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>, <a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">45</a>]</cite>, the preference score can be obtained in several ways: (1) producing multiple binary judgments through repeated queries and averaging the outcomes; (2) prompting the model to directly output a numerical preference score; or (3) extracting log-probabilities assigned to preference tokens (e.g., <span id="S4.SS5.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">1</span> for <math id="S4.SS5.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{1}\succ y_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>≻</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{1}\succ y_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, <span id="S4.SS5.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">-1</span> for <math id="S4.SS5.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{2}\succ y_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>≻</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{2}\succ y_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>), followed by a softmax transformation to estimate a distribution over preferences <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite>. Similarly, when an off-the-shelf reward model is used <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>, <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>, <a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>, <a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite>, its scalar outputs can be converted into pairwise preference probabilities using the BT model.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS5.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS5.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In many practical settings, particularly those involving expensive human annotation, only a binary label may be available per <math id="S4.SS5.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x,y_{1},y_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x,y_{1},y_{2})</annotation></semantics></math>. In this case, we observe only one sample from the underlying Bernoulli distribution <math id="S4.SS5.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q(y_{1}\succ y_{2}\mid x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>≻</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q(y_{1}\succ y_{2}\mid x)</annotation></semantics></math>, making estimation of <math id="S4.SS5.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> difficult. Additionally, KL divergence becomes ill-defined when <math id="S4.SS5.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> is exactly 0 or 1 due to a zero denominator. To address this, we slightly relax the chi-squared divergence constraint and rewrite it as
<math id="S4.SS5.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(p-q)^{2}\leq\rho\,q(1-q)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(p-q)^{2}\leq\rho\,q(1-q),</annotation></semantics></math>
which reduces to <math id="S4.SS5.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{p}=q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{p}=q</annotation></semantics></math> when <math id="S4.SS5.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q\in\{0,1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q\in\{0,1\}</annotation></semantics></math>. As a result, when binary labels are used, the DRO loss reduces exactly to the standard DPO loss, introducing no additional regularization. This naturally unifies the treatment of binary and soft labels. Only soft annotations (such as those from LLM judges, reward models, or aggregated human votes) contribute regularization, while binary labels are treated as ground truth.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS5.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS5.p4.1" class="ltx_p">If additional robustness is desired even under binary supervision, one can apply smoothing by replacing label <math id="S4.SS5.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S4.SS5.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1-\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1-\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S4.SS5.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S4.SS5.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S4.SS5.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math> is a small hyperparameter. The DRO formulation can then be applied to this smoothed label. This makes the uncertainty-weighted coefficient uniform across all pairs (since <math id="S4.SS5.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> only takes values <math id="S4.SS5.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1-\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1-\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S4.SS5.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>), but the <math id="S4.SS5.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\ell_{1}-\ell_{-1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\ell_{1}-\ell_{-1})</annotation></semantics></math> term still encourages the model to reduce overconfidence in its current preference.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Experiments</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate <span id="S5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">DPO-PRO</span> in two settings. First, we validate the approach on a standardized benchmark for preference-based LLM fine-tuning. Second, we evaluate the method on a reward function design task for a real-world public health resource allocation problem.</p>
</div>
<section id="S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.1 </span>Benchmarks on General Alignment Datasets</h3>

<div id="S5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We use the recent UltraFeedback alignment benchmark dataset <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite>, consisting of 60,000 top-quality human pairwise preference annotations that cover a wide variety of tasks and instruction types, rendering it a broad benchmark for examining alignment research.</p>
</div>
<section id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Noise in the training data</h4>

<div id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our primary objective is to evaluate how effectively preference-based LLM fine-tuning methods handle shifts in preference distributions between training and evaluation data. We use <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>q</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the true preference distribution used at test time. For our experiments, we define <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>q</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> using the state-of-the-art preference reward model Eurus 7B<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">42</a>]</cite>, which has been trained for the UltraFeedback dataset <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite>. We compute <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>q</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> using the BT model.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To simulate noise during training, we construct a noisy preference distribution <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math> for training as the mixture</p>
<table id="S5.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{\alpha}:=q^{*}(1-\alpha)+(1-q^{*})\alpha." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>q</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>q</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{\alpha}:=q^{*}(1-\alpha)+(1-q^{*})\alpha.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">Intuitively, <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math> “flips" the preference distribution with probability <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>. We refer to this type of noise interchangeably as <span id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">label switching</span>. During training, we have access to <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math> but not to <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>q</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>, with <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> being unknown.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Training details</h4>

<div id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We base our experiments on the recent Phi 3-mini 3B model without instruction <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite>. We initially train for two epochs of supervised fine tuning (SFT), followed by one epoch of DPO, with the baselines discussed below. All experiments are performed on a Nvidia A100 GPU 40 GB with batch size 2. Refer to the technical appendix for full details on the training procedure.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Baselines</h4>

<div id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We compare <span id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">DPO-PRO</span> against the standard DPO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>]</cite> and the prior distributionally robust variant DrDPO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">36</a>]</cite>. We evaluate <span id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">DPO-PRO</span> under several values of the robustness parameter <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math> based on the <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>χ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>-divergence. Models are trained under two levels of label-flip noise, <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=0.3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=0.3</annotation></semantics></math> (low) and <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=0.6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=0.6</annotation></semantics></math> (high), as well as the noiseless setting <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=0</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Evaluation metrics</h4>

<div id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">After the fine-tuning step, we generate a response <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{g}\sim\pi_{\theta}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{g}\sim\pi_{\theta}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> for every prompt <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> in the evaluation dataset and compute its reward <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{\phi}(x,y_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{\phi}(x,y_{g})</annotation></semantics></math>. We consider three evaluation metrics. The first is the <em id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">win rate</em>, computed by comparing the generated response with the chosen response <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{c}</annotation></semantics></math> in the dataset as
<math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\texttt{win\_rate}=\frac{1}{N_{\mathrm{eval}}}\sum_{i=1}^{N_{\mathrm{eval}}}\mathbb{I}\big(R_{\phi}(x,y_{g})&gt;R_{\phi}(x,y_{c})\big)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">win_rate</mtext><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>eval</mi></msub></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>eval</mi></msub></msubsup><mrow><mi>𝕀</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\texttt{win\_rate}=\frac{1}{N_{\mathrm{eval}}}\sum_{i=1}^{N_{\mathrm{eval}}}\mathbb{I}\big(R_{\phi}(x,y_{g})&gt;R_{\phi}(x,y_{c})\big),</annotation></semantics></math>
where <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝕀</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{I}</annotation></semantics></math> is the indicator function.
The second metric, <em id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">generation evaluation reward</em>, is defined as
<math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\texttt{eval\_reward}=\frac{1}{N_{\mathrm{eval}}}\sum_{i=1}^{N_{\mathrm{eval}}}R_{\phi}(x,y_{g})." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">eval_reward</mtext><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>eval</mi></msub></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>eval</mi></msub></msubsup><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\texttt{eval\_reward}=\frac{1}{N_{\mathrm{eval}}}\sum_{i=1}^{N_{\mathrm{eval}}}R_{\phi}(x,y_{g}).</annotation></semantics></math>
Finally, we also evaluate responses using an LLM-as-a-judge approach with GPT-4o-mini <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>]</cite>. To mitigate position bias, we randomly flip the order of the two responses presented to the judge.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px5" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Results</h4>

<div id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#S5.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ Results ‣ 5.1 Benchmarks on General Alignment Datasets ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ Lightweight Robust Direct Preference Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> summarizes the results. For metrics based on reward models, <span id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">DPO-PRO</span> outperforms the baselines across all noise levels, including the noiseless case. As expected, performance declines as noise increases for all methods. The effect of the robustness parameter <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math> is also consistent with intuition: smaller <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math> values tend to perform better under low noise but exhibit reduced robustness under higher noise, whereas larger <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math> values maintain more stable performance.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For the LLM-as-a-judge evaluation, <span id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px5.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">DPO-PRO</span> generally underperforms when noise is small but improves relative to other methods as the noise level increases. Under high noise, <span id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px5.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">DPO-PRO</span> surpasses both vanilla DPO and DrDPO, reflecting the same robustness trend observed with the reward-based metrics.</p>
</div>
