<section id="A1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix A </span>Codebase</h2>

<div id="A1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We release the codebase for all our experiments on finetuning LLMs here: <a href="https://anonymous.4open.science/r/DPO-Pro-A33D/README.md" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0000FF;">https://anonymous.4open.science/r/DPO-Pro-A33D/README.md</a></p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix B </span>Extensions and Generalizations</h2>

<section id="A2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.1 </span>Beyond the Chi-squared Divergence</h3>

<div id="A2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We use the chi-squared divergence to define the ambiguity set due to the simplicity of its resulting closed-form solution. However, alternative divergence measures can also be used and are likely to offer comparable computational efficiency as ours. This is because the per-sample optimization problem in Eq (<a href="#S4.E2" title="In 4.2 Computing Worst-case Distribution ‣ 4 DPO with Preference Robustness ‣ Lightweight Robust Direct Preference Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>) remains a one-dimensional problem with linear objective. For example, using the KL divergence leads to a constraint of the form: <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\log\frac{p}{q}+(1-p)\log\frac{1-p}{1-q}\;\leq\;\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mfrac><mi>p</mi><mi>q</mi></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">≤</mo><mi>ρ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\log\frac{p}{q}+(1-p)\log\frac{1-p}{1-q}\;\leq\;\rho</annotation></semantics></math>. In this case, the worst-case <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{p}</annotation></semantics></math> can be computed by solving the equation <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\log\frac{p}{q}+(1-p)\log\frac{1-p}{1-q}\;=\;\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mfrac><mi>p</mi><mi>q</mi></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mi>ρ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\log\frac{p}{q}+(1-p)\log\frac{1-p}{1-q}\;=\;\rho</annotation></semantics></math>, which can be efficiently handled using a simple one-dimensional root-finding method.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A3" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix C </span>Omitted Proofs</h2>

<section id="A3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">C.1 </span>Proof of Proposition <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem1" title="Proposition 4.1. ‣ 4.3 Efficient Optimization of the Loss ‣ 4 DPO with Preference Robustness ‣ Lightweight Robust Direct Preference Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a></h3>

<div id="A3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The result follows from Danskin’s theorem <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite>. To apply the theorem, we must verify that the inner maximization problem in Eq. (<a href="#S4.E2" title="In 4.2 Computing Worst-case Distribution ‣ 4 DPO with Preference Robustness ‣ Lightweight Robust Direct Preference Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>) has a compact feasible set and admits a unique optimal solution.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To establish compactness, we need to show that the feasible set is both bounded and closed. Boundedness is immediate since <math id="A3.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>. Closedness follows from the fact that the constraint function <math id="A3.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\mapsto(p-q)^{2}-\rho q(1-q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\mapsto(p-q)^{2}-\rho q(1-q)</annotation></semantics></math> is continous in <math id="A3.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence, the set <math id="A3.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(p-q)^{2}\leq\rho q(1-q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(p-q)^{2}\leq\rho q(1-q)</annotation></semantics></math> is closed. Since <math id="A3.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> is also closed and the intersection of closed sets is closed, the feasible set is closed. Uniqueness of the optimal solution <math id="A3.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{p}</annotation></semantics></math> is also straightforward, since the objective is one-dimensional linear function in <math id="A3.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">C.2 </span>Proof of Proposition <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem2" title="Proposition 4.2. ‣ 4.4 Analysis of the Loss Function ‣ 4 DPO with Preference Robustness ‣ Lightweight Robust Direct Preference Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a></h3>

<div id="A3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We begin by assuming the case <math id="A3.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell_{1}\geq\ell_{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell_{1}\geq\ell_{-1}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then,</p>
<table id="A3.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A3.Ex8"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{L}_{\text{DRO}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>DRO</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{L}_{\text{DRO}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.Ex8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\hat{p}\,\ell_{1}+(1-\hat{p})\,\ell_{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\hat{p}\,\ell_{1}+(1-\hat{p})\,\ell_{-1}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A3.Ex9"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=q\,\ell_{1}+(1-q)\,\ell_{-1}+(\hat{p}-q)(\ell_{1}-\ell_{-1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>−</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=q\,\ell_{1}+(1-q)\,\ell_{-1}+(\hat{p}-q)(\ell_{1}-\ell_{-1})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A3.Ex10"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\mathcal{L}_{\text{DPO}}+\underbrace{\min\left\{1-q,\;\sqrt{\rho\,q(1-q)}\right\}}_{\text{Uncertainty-Weighted Coefficient}}(\ell_{1}-\ell_{-1})." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>DPO</mtext></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><munder><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><mtext>Uncertainty-Weighted Coefficient</mtext></munder><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\mathcal{L}_{\text{DPO}}+\underbrace{\min\left\{1-q,\;\sqrt{\rho\,q(1-q)}\right\}}_{\text{Uncertainty-Weighted Coefficient}}(\ell_{1}-\ell_{-1}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">For the opposite case, when <math id="A3.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell_{-1}&gt;\ell_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell_{-1}&gt;\ell_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, the derivation proceeds symmetrically.</p>
<table id="A3.EGx2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A3.Ex12"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.Ex12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{L}_{\text{DRO}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>DRO</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{L}_{\text{DRO}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.Ex12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\hat{p}\,\ell_{1}+(1-\hat{p})\,\ell_{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\hat{p}\,\ell_{1}+(1-\hat{p})\,\ell_{-1}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A3.Ex13"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.Ex13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\mathcal{L}_{\text{DPO}}+(\hat{p}-q)(\ell_{1}-\ell_{-1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>DPO</mtext></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>−</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\mathcal{L}_{\text{DPO}}+(\hat{p}-q)(\ell_{1}-\ell_{-1})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A3.Ex14"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.Ex14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\mathcal{L}_{\text{DPO}}-\min\left\{q,\,\sqrt{\rho\,q(1-q)}\right\}(\ell_{1}-\ell_{-1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>DPO</mtext></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mi>q</mi><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msqrt><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\mathcal{L}_{\text{DPO}}-\min\left\{q,\,\sqrt{\rho\,q(1-q)}\right\}(\ell_{1}-\ell_{-1})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A3.Ex15"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.Ex15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\mathcal{L}_{\text{DPO}}+\min\left\{q,\,\sqrt{\rho\,q(1-q)}\right\}(\ell_{-1}-\ell_{1})." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>DPO</mtext></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mi>q</mi><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msqrt><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\mathcal{L}_{\text{DPO}}+\min\left\{q,\,\sqrt{\rho\,q(1-q)}\right\}(\ell_{-1}-\ell_{1}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A4" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix D </span>Experimental Details</h2>

<section id="A4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">D.1 </span>Description of the Baseline</h3>

<div id="A4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">DrDPO applies DRO over the empirical data distribution using a KL-divergence-based ambiguity set:</p>
<table id="A4.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A4.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\min_{\pi_{\theta}}\max_{P\in\mathcal{Q}(\mathcal{D})}\mathbb{E}_{(x,y_{1},y_{2},c)\sim P}[\mathcal{L}_{\text{DPO}}(\pi_{\theta})]," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mi>min</mi><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><munder><mi>max</mi><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></munder></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∼</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>DPO</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\min_{\pi_{\theta}}\max_{P\in\mathcal{Q}(\mathcal{D})}\mathbb{E}_{(x,y_{1},y_{2},c)\sim P}[\mathcal{L}_{\text{DPO}}(\pi_{\theta})],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A4.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A4.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{Q}(\mathcal{D}):=\left\{P:D_{\text{KL}}(P\|\mathcal{D})\leq\rho\right\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mtext>KL</mtext></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Q}(\mathcal{D}):=\left\{P:D_{\text{KL}}(P\|\mathcal{D})\leq\rho\right\}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Using duality, the inner maximum (the objective to minimize) is approximated by:</p>
<table id="A4.E7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A4.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta^{\prime}\log\mathbb{E}_{(x,y_{1},y_{2},c)\sim\mathcal{D}}\left[\exp\left(\frac{1}{\beta^{\prime}}\mathcal{L}_{\text{DPO}}(\pi_{\theta})\right)\right]," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>β</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∼</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msup><mi>β</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>DPO</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta^{\prime}\log\mathbb{E}_{(x,y_{1},y_{2},c)\sim\mathcal{D}}\left[\exp\left(\frac{1}{\beta^{\prime}}\mathcal{L}_{\text{DPO}}(\pi_{\theta})\right)\right],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A4.SS1.p1.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A4.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>β</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is a hyperparameter.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">D.2 </span>Multi-armed Restless Bandits</h3>

<div id="A4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A Multi-Armed Restless Bandit problem is characterized by N Markov Decision processes, each defined by the tuple <math id="A4.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\mathcal{S},\mathcal{A},\mathcal{R},\mathcal{T},\gamma\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\mathcal{S},\mathcal{A},\mathcal{R},\mathcal{T},\gamma\}</annotation></semantics></math>. Here, <math id="A4.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math> represents the set of states, <math id="A4.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}</annotation></semantics></math> represents the set of actions which are binary in <math id="A4.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{0,1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{0,1\}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A4.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}:\mathcal{S}\times\mathcal{A}\times\mathcal{S}\to[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}:\mathcal{S}\times\mathcal{A}\times\mathcal{S}\to[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> represents the transition probability from current state, under an action to the next state. <math id="A4.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}:\mathcal{S}\to\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}:\mathcal{S}\to\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> represents a reward function mapping state to a real number, and <math id="A4.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math> represents the discounting factor.
We also have a budget <math id="A4.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> which represents the number of arms that can be chosen at every timestep.
The objective of the Multi-Armed Restless Bandit problem then is to decide a vector of actions <math id="A4.SS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textbf{A}^{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">A</mtext><mi>t</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textbf{A}^{t}</annotation></semantics></math> representing actions <math id="A4.SS2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{i}^{t}\in\mathcal{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{i}^{t}\in\mathcal{A}</annotation></semantics></math> for every arm i in time step <math id="A4.SS2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> such that we maximize long term reward under the budget constraint:</p>
<table id="A4.Ex16" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A4.Ex16.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="J(s)=\max_{\textbf{A}\in\mathcal{A}}\left(\sum_{i=1}^{N}R_{i}(s_{i})+\gamma\mathbb{E}[J(s^{\prime})\mid s,\textbf{A}]\right)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>J</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><munder><mi>max</mi><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">A</mtext><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><mo>(</mo><munderover><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></munderover><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>γ</mi><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>J</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">A</mtext><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J(s)=\max_{\textbf{A}\in\mathcal{A}}\left(\sum_{i=1}^{N}R_{i}(s_{i})+\gamma\mathbb{E}[J(s^{\prime})\mid s,\textbf{A}]\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<table id="A4.Ex17" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A4.Ex17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s.t.\sum_{i=1}^{i=N}A_{i}^{t}\leq K\;\forall\;t=1\;\text{to}\;T" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">.</mo><mrow><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></munderover><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0.447em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">∀</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.280em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>to</mtext><mo lspace="0.280em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s.t.\sum_{i=1}^{i=N}A_{i}^{t}\leq K\;\forall\;t=1\;\text{to}\;T</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A4.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A4.SS2.p1.m12" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="s_{t}\sim T(s_{t-1},a_{t},.)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><mi>T</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{t}\sim T(s_{t-1},a_{t},.)</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">D.3 </span>Whittle Index Policy</h3>

<div id="A4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Whittle index is a heuristic that is asymptotically optimal for planning in Multi-Armed Restless Bandits.
To calculate whittle index, first we define the value function as</p>
<table id="A4.EGx3" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A4.E8"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A4.E8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle V_{i}^{\lambda}(s)=\max_{a\in\{0,1\}}Q_{i}(s,a_{i},\lambda)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>λ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><munder><mi>max</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle V_{i}^{\lambda}(s)=\max_{a\in\{0,1\}}Q_{i}(s,a_{i},\lambda).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(8)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A4.SS3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Here, <math id="A4.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{i}(s,a_{i},\lambda)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{i}(s,a_{i},\lambda)</annotation></semantics></math> measures the expected discounted cumulative future reward where a reward compensation <math id="A4.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> is added to the reward when the passive action is taken. The Whittle index associated to the state <math id="A4.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is then defined as:</p>
<table id="A4.EGx4" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A4.Ex18"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A4.Ex18.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle W_{i}(s_{i}):=\inf_{m}\left\{Q_{i}(s_{i},a_{i}=0,m)=Q_{i}(s_{i},a_{i}=1,m)\right\}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">:=</mo><munder><mo lspace="0.1389em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">inf</mo><mi>m</mi></munder><mrow><mo>{</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle W_{i}(s_{i}):=\inf_{m}\left\{Q_{i}(s_{i},a_{i}=0,m)=Q_{i}(s_{i},a_{i}=1,m)\right\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A4.SS3.p1.3" class="ltx_p">The Whittle Index intuitively captures the value of taking an active action on an arm. We compute the Whittle Index using a binary search algorithm proposed by <span id="A4.SS3.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Qian et al. 2016.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A4.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Finally, as a policy, we choose at every timestep the beneficiaires with top-K whittle indices.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A4.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">D.4 </span>Details on the Public Health Experiment</h3>

<div id="A4.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We apply our proposed method in the context of mobile health program run by an NGO ARMMAN <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>. Specifically, we have anonymized beneficiary listenership data from a quality-improvement study conducted by ARMMAN<span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">1</span>
              
              
              
            The authors have been access to this restricted dataset under a data usage agreement.</span></span></span>. The data consists of registration information of beneficiaries (mothers) containing their socio-demographic features of age, income education as well as their preferences on call slot times and language. Additionally, we have information on beneficiaries’ interaction with automated voice calls and live service calls made by health-workers. We model the problem of allocating live service calls as a RMAB problem. For all our experiments, we consider a sample of population with <math id="A4.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2100" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2100</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2100</annotation></semantics></math> beneficiaries and <math id="A4.SS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K=210" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>210</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K=210</annotation></semantics></math> as the budget, representing the number of live service calls that can be made every week. Exact policy optimization for RMABs is generally known to be intractable <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>]</cite>. However, Whittle index policy <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>]</cite> is a popular heuristic to obtain an asymptotically optimal policy.</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To conduct our experiments in the ARMMAN real-world setting, we use a dataset of 2100 beneficiaries as a subset from a quality improvement study conducted in January 2022. Specifically, we use transition probabilities of beneficiaries estimated from historical data and use these probabilities to simulate beneficiary behaviour. Table <a href="#A4.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ D.4 Details on the Public Health Experiment ‣ Appendix D Experimental Details ‣ Lightweight Robust Direct Preference Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> shows the beneficiary attributes we use as the set of features that LLM can use in the reward function. This includes features such as sociodemographic attributes, registration information, as well as preference on time slots of calls and on the language in which they want to receive calls.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A4.T4" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="A4.T4.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table 4</span>: </span><span id="A4.T4.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Features describing beneficiaries in the ARMMAN dataset</span></figcaption>
<table id="A4.T4.5" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="A4.T4.5.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="A4.T4.5.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><span id="A4.T4.5.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Feature Name</span></th>
<th id="A4.T4.5.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><span id="A4.T4.5.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Data Type</span></th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="A4.T4.5.2" class="ltx_tr">
